# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the nightly `tsan` shard.
#
# Scope discipline, same as lsan.supp: every entry here names a *third-party*
# synchronisation primitive that ThreadSanitizer cannot model, never a frame in
# `alkahest_cas`. If a report has an Alkahest frame in it, it does not belong in
# this file — fix the code.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# crossbeam-epoch (reached via crossbeam-deque → rayon's work-stealing deques)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Epoch-based reclamation coordinates the reader (`Guard`) and the reclaimer
# (`Global::collect` → `try_advance` → `Bag::drop` → `free`) with
# `atomic::fence(SeqCst)` plus, on Linux, an *asymmetric* barrier (a
# `membarrier(2)` syscall, or the `mprotect` fallback) issued on the reclaiming
# side only. ThreadSanitizer models neither: it sees the reclaimer's `free` and
# a reader's relaxed `Atomic::load` of the same word with no happens-before edge
# it can account for, and reports a race.
#
# The reports observed here are entirely inside crossbeam/rayon — the two stacks
# are `rayon_core::registry::main_loop → WorkerThread::steal →
# crossbeam_epoch::pin → Global::collect` on both sides, with no Alkahest frame
# anywhere. This is a long-standing known limitation (crossbeam-rs/crossbeam
# has carried it for years), not a defect in the parallel simplifier: with these
# suppressed, `alkahest-core/tests/parallel_stress.rs` — which drives concurrent
# `ExprPool` interning, concurrent `simplify_par` / `simplify_redex` on one
# shared pool, and nested `call_batch_par` from real OS threads — runs clean.
#
# Deliberately narrow: only the epoch/deque machinery, so a genuine race in a
# Rayon closure of ours still fails the shard.
race:crossbeam_epoch::
race:crossbeam_deque::
